  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A more typical summertime pattern returns to \ncentral North Carolina over the next week, with diurnally-driven \nscattered thunderstorms and highs in the 90s.\n\nAs opposed to the last several days, where showers and thunderstorm \ncoverage has been more widespread and highs have been in the 80s, \nthe coverage of precipitation should be less over the next week. \nPrecipitable water values have been well over 2 inches, and will \ndrop closer to 2 inches, which is at or slightly above normal for \nthis time of year. In addition, there will be fewer synoptic \nfeatures serving to force any precipitation. Any frontal boundaries \nshould remain closer to the US/Canadian border, and after a broad \nupper trough moves over the East Coast this weekend, the mid level \nflow becomes more zonal. Each day there will likely be isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms, but overall, less people will be receiving \nrain each day. The surface wind will generally be light and out of \nthe southwest, which should allow some warmer air to eventually \nfilter into the region. Friday is the first day in the forecast \nwhere most locations will reach the lower 90s, and mid 90s don't \nappear likely to return to the majority of the region until at least \nTuesday. Lows will be in the upper 60s and lower 70s.",
